# What every device states, and why any of them states something else.
#
# A device says what it is by the entries on its class. The machinery
# above it reads them and sends it different work accordingly: a device
# offering a process colour model is moved to it by a page-device
# request, one asking for a glyph's edge pixels as coverage is sent
# blends rather than whole pixels, one that renders a grey as a pattern
# is told which screen is in force. So an entry is a question the family
# has been asked, and every device answers it -- including the ones
# whose author never heard it, which answer by inheriting whatever the
# class they were copied from said.
#
# This is the register of those entries. tests/check-device-facts.sh
# installs every device in a running interpreter, reads its dictionary
# and holds what it finds to what is written here, in both directions:
# an entry no line classifies fails, and a line naming an entry no
# device carries fails. The devices carrying an entry are named and are
# held to the ones that do.
#
# The point of the first direction is that a new mechanism cannot be
# added quietly. Every mechanism has to show up as something -- a key on
# a class is what a device fact shows up as -- so enumerating what the
# devices carry and refusing anything unaccounted for stops the author
# of the next one here until they say which kind it is. They do not have
# to remember; they cannot proceed without it.
#
# ---- the kinds
#
#   question   every device carrying it answers, and the answers are
#              written down. One answer is the family's; a device
#              answering otherwise names the file it states its own
#              answer in and says why, in prose that says what would
#              make it false. An answer that has come back to the
#              family's may not keep its reason.
#
#   elsewhere  a fact another guard already holds. The line says which,
#              so that the same fact is not held twice and differently.
#
#   open       a fact no guard holds and this does not yet ask as a
#              family question. The line says why not and what making it
#              one would cost. This is the register's own residue, kept
#              where it can be read rather than left as silence.
#
#   method     a call the device takes. Which calls a device must offer
#              is held by tests/check-device-skeleton.sh and the raster
#              slot completeness beside it; what is held here is which
#              devices offer each, so a method spreading to a device
#              that should not have one, or leaving one that should,
#              fails.
#
#   state      what an instance carries while it works. Not a statement
#              about the device: two devices holding different values
#              here are not differing about anything.
#
#   part       a constant or table the class reads only itself.
#
# ---- reading a line
#
#   <kind> <entry> <device>...
#
# naming every device whose class carries the entry, or `every` for the
# whole roster. A device this build cannot make states nothing and is
# left out of both sides rather than counted absent.
#
# The recorder is a device that paints nothing: it stands in front of one
# that does, writes down what it was asked to paint and plays it back.
# Its answers are its target's, so it is named here only for the entries
# it carries whatever it stands in front of. The rest are its target's
# showing through, and the guard holds those to the target directly by
# making a record over each target in turn.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The traits
#
# Two things about a device are settled by how it was built rather than
# by anything it says, so neither can be read off a class the way the
# entries below are. They are derived from the tree and held to these
# lines in both directions.

trait compiled bgr jpeg png pngalpha raster record
    That there is compiled code behind this device. The PostScript
    classes are the family's ordinary shape -- a class dictionary in
    data/ saying what it paints with -- and a compiled driver is a
    departure from it, so what wants saying is why each of the six
    departs.
    Four of them hold a raster of their own in a layout no PostScript
    string is: png and jpeg hand rows straight to a codec that wants
    them in its own arrangement, bgr keeps a byte order a display
    expects, and raster keeps four such arrangements chosen per
    instance. The record keeps a mark list rather than a raster and
    replays it, which is a data structure with no PostScript spelling
    that would not cost a round trip per mark.
    None of the six is compiled for speed in the abstract. Each is
    compiled because the thing it holds is not a PostScript object, and
    the day one of them holds only what PostScript can hold is the day
    it stops needing a driver. Note that this is a claim about the
    pixels, not about the page: four of the six devices that take a page
    in bands are PostScript classes with no driver at all.
disposition settled

trait pagecodec jpeg png
    That this device drives its page out through a stream codec, which
    is the descriptor of four calls in xpost_dev_driver.h -- open the
    stream, write the rows, finish it, drop it -- rather than writing
    the bytes itself.
    Only these two compress. A codec that wants rows fed to it in order
    and finished before the file means anything is a shape the plain
    writers do not have: pgm, ppm, pbm and tiff put their bytes down as
    they are, so the machinery that would carry them through a codec
    would be carrying them past nothing.
    So the descriptor has exactly the clients it was written for, and
    the four other devices that take a page in bands do not want one. It
    is not the band route that calls for a codec; it is compression.
disposition settled

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The questions

question .colormodels pdfwrite dscwrite
    The process colour models a page-device request may move this device
    to. data/device.ps reads it: a request naming a model this device
    offers makes that model the device's native space, and the colour
    pipeline then delivers that model's components to the marking
    methods. A device that states nothing offers nothing but the space
    it already has, which is the safe answer -- a request it cannot
    honour changes nothing rather than sending it components it has no
    method for.
    There is no family answer. What a device can offer is what its
    output can express, and no two of these express the same set by
    accident: each states its own, and states it where it is, so that a
    claim made about one device is never made on behalf of another.
family per-device
answer pdfwrite set:DeviceCMYK,DeviceRGB,Process data/pdfwrite.ps
    A PDF content stream names a paint in the space it was set in and
    carries an operator for each of the three: g and G for grey, rg and
    RG for rgb, k and K for cmyk. So a press workflow receives pure-K
    ink as pure K rather than a converted black.
    This stops being true if the content writer stops emitting one of
    those operators, or if a reader of the stream is no longer given
    the space a paint was written in.
answer dscwrite set:DeviceCMYK,DeviceRGB,Process data/dscwrite.ps
    The same three, on this device's own ground rather than on the class
    it copies: its prolog defines g, rg and k over setgray, setrgbcolor
    and setcmykcolor, so a paint made in any of the three is written in
    the space it was made in and read back in that space.
    This stops being true if the prolog stops defining one of them,
    which would leave the claim standing over an operator no reader is
    given.
disposition settled

question .groundvaries bgr jpeg pbm pgm png pngalpha ppm raster tiff record
    Whether the row this device shows over a row of the page it holds no
    pixel of is the same row whatever the row is. A class storing the
    colour it is handed shows one row of that colour wherever it shows
    the ground; one that varies has to fill a row for the row it stands
    for, and data/image.ps then puts its page out through the walk a
    band's rows go out through rather than laying one row over every row
    it does not hold.
    The family answer is that it does not vary, which is the cheap
    answer and the one a device holding a whole page of its own never
    has to think about. Answering otherwise costs a fill per unheld row
    and is only worth it where the ground genuinely differs down the
    page.
    A device that shows a grey as a pattern of pixels -- ScreenPaint,
    below -- answers this true and is held to it there, since the cell
    it ranks against is whatever screen the program set and no class can
    promise one row over the page while that is so. The entries stay
    apart because the implication is one-way and the two are read for
    different things: this by the walk that puts a page out row by row,
    that by the machinery that tells a device which screen is in force.
family false
answer pgm false
answer ppm false
answer tiff false
answer raster false
answer bgr false
answer png false
answer pngalpha false
answer jpeg false
answer pbm true data/pbmimage.ps
    This device stores a grey by ranking the pixel against a threshold
    that the screen in force picks from the pixel's position, and the
    cell tiles the page. So the ground is one colour and what stands for
    it at one row is not what stands for it at the next.
    This stops being true if the device stops screening -- it is the
    tiling that makes the row depend on which row it is, and a device
    storing the grey it is handed would show the same row everywhere.
disposition settled

question TextAlphaBits bgr jpeg pbm pgm png pngalpha ppm raster tiff record
    How many bits of a glyph's edge coverage this device is sent. Above
    one, the text machinery (src/lib/xpost_op_font.c) blends a partly
    covered edge pixel against what the page already holds, at the
    coverage rounded to the nearest of the values that many bits tell
    apart; at one it sends whole pixels and the edge is hard. The
    number is the number it says: two bits is four values of coverage
    and four bits is sixteen, and the greys a page of text holds can be
    counted to see it. A page-device request may name it -- as an
    integer, which setpagedevice refuses it for not being
    (data/device.ps) -- and a program may write it onto the device
    directly, so what a class states is where a run starts rather than
    where it ends. Except on a device that shows a grey as a pattern of
    pixels, which is sent whole pixels whatever the number says
    (ScreenPaint, below).
    The family answer is eight, which is every value there is: a
    rasterised glyph carries one byte of coverage a pixel, so eight bits
    is the whole of what a device can be sent and a device asking for
    more is answered with that. A device answering otherwise is one
    whose stored pixel holds fewer values than the coverage has, and
    sending it more would have it round each of them and show an edge it
    never agreed to.
family 8
answer pgm 8
answer ppm 8
answer tiff 8
answer raster 8
answer bgr 8
answer png 8
answer pngalpha 8
answer jpeg 8
answer pbm 1 data/pbmimage.ps
    A pixel of this device is one bit. There is no value between the two
    it can store, so a coverage-weighted blend has nowhere to land, and
    the blend does not pass through the pixel store the screen is
    applied in: what it writes is the weighted value itself, to exactly
    the pixels a glyph's edges cover, and those are the pixels the
    screen would otherwise have decided. A page that took them would
    hold a range of values this device cannot show and would report
    them back to a program reading a pixel.
    So the number here is not only what the class starts at. The text
    machinery reads it together with ScreenPaint and sends whole pixels
    to a device declaring one, which is what makes a run naming a
    larger number reach the same page as a run naming none.
    This stops being true if the device grows a pixel that can hold an
    intermediate value, which is the same change that would let it show
    a grey without screening for one.
disposition settled

question ScreenPaint pbm
    Whether a grey reaches this device as a pattern of pixels rather
    than as a value. data/gstate.ps reads it: a device declaring it is
    given the cell built from the current screen and is kept up to date
    as the screen changes, since the screen a mark was made under is
    state a marking call does not otherwise carry.
    There is no family answer. Screening is a property of what a device
    can store, not a setting, and a device that does not screen is never
    told of a screen and holds none -- which is the safe answer, since a
    device holding a cell it never reads would be carrying page state
    that nothing keeps right.
    One device carries this and one carries .groundvaries, and they are
    not the same entry said twice. This one is about how a colour is
    stored; that one is about whether a row the device holds no pixel of
    can be shown by one row of bytes wherever it stands. What relates
    them runs one way only: a class that screens cannot promise the
    second, because the cell is the screen in force and the screen is
    the program's to set, so a device that screens must answer
    .groundvaries true whatever cell it is holding today. The converse
    is not implied -- a device could vary its ground for a reason of its
    own and store the grey it is handed -- so folding the two would make
    the band walk read a question about storage to answer a question
    about rows. The implication is held instead: check-device-facts.sh
    fails a device that declares this and does not answer .groundvaries
    true, over the roster and over the record standing in for it.
family per-device
answer pbm true data/pbmimage.ps
    Every grey painted on this device screens through the halftone in
    force: the cell ranks the pixels it tiles, and a grey whitens the
    pixel it covers where it reaches the threshold under that pixel.
    This stops being true if the device grows a pixel that can store a
    grey directly, at which point the screen would be a choice about
    rendering rather than the only way it can show one.
disposition settled

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Facts another guard holds

elsewhere BandedPage jpeg pbm pgm png ppm tiff record
    Whether this device's page may arrive a band at a time.
    tests/check-device-roster.sh holds it against .playtargets and the
    C table the selection is rewritten from, so a device declaring it
    and not routed for it, or routed and not declaring it, fails there;
    tests/check-device-skeleton.sh holds each compiled class to saying
    it again or taking it back out rather than inheriting the answer.
    What is held here is only which devices carry it.
disposition thorn
    What is written down is which devices band, and by what route. Why
    the others do not is not written down anywhere, and for two of them
    it is not obvious: raster and bgr keep a raster of their own and
    decline, while four of the six that band -- pgm, ppm, pbm and tiff
    -- are PostScript classes with no compiled help. So banding is not
    something the compiled devices are better placed to do, and the two
    that hold a buffer and refuse are unexplained rather than excused.
    Removing this costs a reason from each of raster and bgr, and a
    measurement behind either reason that claims a cost; where no reason
    survives being written, it costs the band route on that device
    instead.

elsewhere .rowcost bgr jpeg pbm pgm png pngalpha ppm tiff record
    What one row of this device's raster costs, as the elements and the
    bytes it takes. tests/check-device-skeleton.sh holds every class
    derived from one that prices a row to pricing its own or taking the
    price back out with the reason, in C and in PostScript alike.
    What is held here is only which devices carry it.
disposition settled

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Facts no guard holds and this does not yet ask

open nativecolorspace every
    The colour space this device's marking methods take components in.
    Every device states one and no two of them are wrong to differ: the
    space is what the device IS, not a way it varies from a family. It
    decides how many components a paint carries (data/paint.ps), which
    compiled fills the device is given (data/device.ps), and what a
    glyph's colour is converted to (src/lib/xpost_op_font.c), so a
    device answering with a space it does not have is one whose whole
    colour pipeline was settled elsewhere.
    Every device now owns the space it answers with. Three of them
    inherited it and three now say it again -- the bilevel class, the
    TIFF class and the DSC writer -- so the shape a claim made on
    another device's behalf takes is gone from this entry whether or not
    it is ever asked as a question.
    It is not asked as one because there is no family for a device to
    differ from. A question is worth asking where devices ought to agree
    and one does not; here fifteen devices would each carry a reason for
    differing from nothing, and a reason that cannot be wrong is a
    reason nobody rereads.
    What would make it one: a device whose space can move after it is
    installed and answers differently from the family because of where
    it was moved to. That happens where a device offers process colour
    models -- .colormodels above -- since a page-device request naming
    one writes this key (data/device.ps). Today only the two document
    writers offer any, and they carry no raster to disagree with. The
    count beside it moves with it, so a raster class declaring
    .colormodels would be moved to a space and sent that space's
    components; what it would still owe is a raster shaped for them,
    which is the device's own business and not a family answer.
    Two of the answers are not a name but a procedure: the compiled
    drivers store the space as an executable name, which is why
    data/device.ps fetches it with get and holds it literal rather than
    naming it again.
disposition settled

open .ncomp bgr jpeg pbm pgm png pngalpha ppm raster tiff record
    How many components a paint carries to this device's marking
    methods. It follows from nativecolorspace -- one for grey, three for
    rgb -- and data/image.ps derives the colour take and push helpers
    from it when it builds a class.
    It is not a question here for the reason above: it is the device
    rather than a difference within a family. It is also the one entry
    a record refuses outright rather than copying, since a record whose
    component count disagreed with its target would replay a colour
    nobody named (src/lib/xpost_dev_record.c).
    It follows the space rather than standing beside it: the
    page-device request that moves a device's colour space writes this
    key too, from the components that space takes, wherever the device
    states one and the space has a fixed count to give
    (.completedevice, data/device.ps; tests/device_completion_test.ps).
    A device offering the model that is whatever space a paint was made
    in has no fixed count and keeps what it had.
    What does not follow is what a raster class derives from this when
    it is built: .takecolor and .pushcolor are written once from the
    count the class was made with (.makerasterclass, data/image.ps), so
    a raster class that ever offered a process colour model would be
    sent the new space's components through helpers shaped for the old
    count. There is no such class, and the day one arrives is the day
    those move too.
    What would make it a question is a device taking a number of
    components its space does not imply -- a raster holding an alpha
    plane beside its colour, say -- at which point the count would stop
    being derivable and each device would have to say what it takes and
    why.
disposition settled

open NoOutput bbox dscwrite null pdfwrite svgwrite record
    That this device puts no pixels down, so data/device.ps leaves it
    the class's own polygon fill rather than substituting the compiled
    scanline rasterizers: rasterizing into a device that discards spans
    would burn the whole render cost of a page to throw it away.
    It is not a question here because it is not a difference of degree
    -- a device either resolves a path into spans or it does not -- and
    the devices carrying it are held by the line above. Making it a
    question would ask nine devices to state a thing that follows from
    whether they carry a raster at all.
    What would make it one is a device on both sides of that: one that
    holds a raster and wants the class's own fill anyway, or one that
    holds none and wants the compiled rasterizers. Either would be a
    device choosing how a path is resolved rather than being told by
    what it is, and the answer would then be worth asking each device
    for. There is none, and the two lists this follows from -- who
    carries a raster, who carries this -- name the same devices.
disposition settled

open VectorGlyphs dscwrite pdfwrite svgwrite
    That text reaches this device as glyph outlines rather than as a
    bitmap of the glyph. The three carrying it are the three that write
    a document rather than a raster, and for them a glyph rendered to
    pixels would be a picture of text in a file whose whole point is
    that the text is still text.
    It is not a question here because it follows from the same thing
    NoOutput follows from. What would make it one is a device that
    writes a document and wants bitmapped glyphs anyway, and there is
    none.
disposition settled

open VectorSyntax svgwrite
    Which vector syntax this device's content is spelled in. Only the
    SVG writer states one; the PDF writer's syntax is the default the
    shared content accumulator was written for, and the DSC writer
    re-spells that same content in its prolog rather than accumulating
    anything else.
    It is not a question here because one device carrying an entry is
    not yet a family answering anything. It becomes one the moment a
    third syntax arrives, and the line above is what will notice.
disposition settled

open AlphaChannel pngalpha
    That this device's page carries transparency, so a page reset clears
    it to transparent rather than to a colour and a read of a pixel
    cannot report what it was cleared to.
    It is not a question here for the reason VectorSyntax is not: one
    device carries it. What it does already do is stand as the reason
    that device is named in the ground test's list of devices with no
    ground to report (tests/run-device-ground-test.sh), which is the one
    place its difference is currently written down.
    What would make it one is a second device whose page carries
    transparency, at which point the two would be answering the same
    question and could answer it differently -- what a page reset clears
    to, and what a read of an untouched pixel may report, are separate
    consequences and a device could take one without the other.
disposition settled

